# RULES — Repository Law & Core Principles

File: openOODA/RULES.oot
These rules do not change. They apply in any project folder.

## 1. Basic rules

### 1.1 First Principles & Ground-Truth Spec Derivation (Pillar 1)
  Find the real cause of a problem. Do not use quick fixes.
  Derive algorithms, data models, and grammar directly from hardware physics, IEEE 754 float behavior, NIST Known Answer Tests (KATs), IETF RFCs, and mathematical invariants.
  Do not mimic existing code or wrap unverified stubs/facades.
  If a claim has no proof, treat it as unproven and broken.

### 1.2 Boyd's E-M Performance Engine (Pillar 2)
  Performance is governed by Col. John Boyd's Energy-Maneuverability formula:
    E-M = ((T - D) / W) * V
  Maximize Thrust (T): Instruction generation throughput, SIMD auto-vectorization, and fast loop dispatch.
  Eliminate Drag (D): Zero heap allocation overhead in hot paths, eliminate ARC retain/release thrashing, and elide dynamic contract evaluation drag.
  Minimize Weight (W): Minimal binary size, minimal stack frame depth, and zero bloated runtime dependencies.
  Accelerate Velocity (V): Microsecond rebuild cycles, fast incremental AST typechecking, and rapid OODA loops.

### 1.3 Act every turn
  Do work on every turn. Do not only look. If you cannot do the work, write why.
  A TODO with no backlog row is not a plan.
  Finish this week's goal before a shiny side project. NORTHSTAR.oot still holds later wants.

### 1.4 Honesty & Zero-orphan delivery
  If a task is not finished, say it is not finished.
  Never leave trailing TODOs, dummy stubs, uncommitted test gates, or unlinked dependencies.
  Every component must be end-to-end verified, double-run certified, and documented in SHIPPED.oot before exiting.

### 1.5 Security & Fail-closed resilience
  All operations must fail closed: zero panics, zero unhandled exceptions, bounded memory, and explicit `Result[T, String]` returns.
  Zero ambient authority: code possesses no implicit access to filesystem, network, process execution, or hardware.
  Follow capability grant matrices in SECURITY.oot. Grant only the exact capability token required (e.g., FsReadCap rather than SysCap).
  Do not put secret keys or passwords in the code or log files.

### 1.6 Multi-Agent Swarm Collaboration & Clean Architecture (Pillar 6)
  The code must get cleaner over time.
  Coordinate across multi-agent concurrent swarms through live git and disk awareness: re-read files before editing and avoid clobbering peer work.
  Make atomic, granular commits with clear handoff reports.
  Do not reach into another module's private internal state.

### 1.7 Proof of work
  When you build a feature, prove that it works.
  Also prove that it fails safely when there is an error.
  A test that always passes is not proof.
  If work fails halfway, leave the world consistent. Do not leave torn state.
  Use hostile probing protocols in WORKFLOW.oot.
  SHIPPED.oot lands only via `scripts/generate_shipped.oo` that proves locks on disk. A failed proof leaves SHIPPED unchanged (fail closed).

### 1.8 Clean code
  A file must not have more than 256 lines. Split large files (see FORMAT.oot).
  Free memory you allocate. Prefer linear arenas with bulk resets.
  Do not leave debug files, old code, or useless text in the folder.
  Delete temporary files when you are done.
  The code must do what the document says it does.

### 1.9 Small steps
  Make small changes. Small changes are easier to test and verify.

### 1.10 Negative Trust & Adversarial Validation (Pillar 3)
  Do not trust other agents or unverified code. Assume stubs, docstrings, and green passes are broken until proven.
  Active Falsification: Subject code to corrupted tokens, invalid curve points, inverted values, and hostile boundary conditions.
  Double-Run Law: Always execute `./bin/ooda run <test.oo>` twice in fresh processes to certify deterministic memory safety and expose ARC double-frees.
  Follow swarm validation and peer review protocols in WORKFLOW.oot.
  Do not trust board text — run `scripts/generate_shipped.oo` and the smoke tests.

### 1.11 Simplified Technical English (ASD-STE100) & Authoritative Clarity
  All agent text and product text must follow ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English.
  Use short sentences. Put one thought in each sentence.
  Use simple words. Give each word one meaning.
  Use the active voice.
  Do not use slang, jokes, metaphors, or meta-framework buzzwords.
  Present mathematical invariants, sovereign capabilities, and architectural advantages with quiet, masterly confidence.
  If you must use a technical word, explain it the first time.
  Tell the user: what changed, why it matters, what is not done, and what to do next.
  FORMAT.oot gives the writing form.

### 1.12 Competition
  If there are two ways to solve a problem, test both.
  The faster or cleaner code wins. Delete the losing code.

### 1.13 Internet rules
  If you write code for the internet, follow the official internet rules (IETF RFCs).
  Write the RFC number in the file.

### 1.14 Pure .oo and .oot Law
  This is a self-hosted OODA project.
  All code, tools, proofs, fixers, test harnesses, QA runners, and scripts are .oo files. Run them with ./bin/ooda or oodac.
  All process boards, research papers, RFCs, specifications, and documentation are .oot files.
  STRICTLY FORBIDDEN:
    - Never create .py files. Python is completely unauthorized across all directories.
    - Never create .sh files, except the two host scripts named below.
    - Never create .js / .ts / .rb / .pl helpers (except vscode glue).
    - Never create .md files (use .oot).
  Folder and name tell the job:
    oodac/*.oo — compiler implementation
    cli/*.oo — CLI entrypoint and commands
    std/*.oo — standard library modules
    fixtures/*.oo — test fixture programs and verification inputs
    ooda/scripts/*.oo — pure .oo proofs, smokes, fixers, and tools
  Form: 4-element Academy header (Title, Logline, Setup, Beats) and strictly <= 256 lines (FORMAT.oot).
  The two host scripts allowed: ../ooda/bootstrap/oodac_pure_build (seed rebuild) and ../openOODA.github.io/install.sh (public installer). No other host shell scripts are allowed.
  `qa/e2e/*.sh` is QA-owned shell, allowed in `qa/` only.

### 1.15 Automated Enforcement
  All agents must run `scripts/enforcer.oo` (or `scripts/verify_all.oo`), `scripts/rfc_lint.oo`, and `scripts/generate_shipped.oo` freshness check before concluding work.
  Any commit or patch introducing unauthorized file extensions (.py, .sh, .md), oversized files (> 256 lines), or non-template RFCs fails closed immediately.

### 1.16 Canonical Board Governance
  All engineering decisions cross-reference the 10 canonical boards:
    - START.oot: Ingress gate, 6 Strategic Pillars, and pre-modification checks.
    - RULES.oot: Immutable laws, Boyd's E-M engine, and code quality axioms.
    - NORTHSTAR.oot: Architecture, AST grammar, and language design.
    - MOONSHOTS.oot: Long-term vision and paradigm shifts.
    - ROADMAP.oot: Release pin, rebuild, closed rows, and later residual tracks.
    - SHIPPED.oot: Generated inventory of verified capabilities on disk. Source of truth is `ooda/` + locks, not the board text.
    - FORMAT.oot: Academy structure, ASD-STE100 writing form, script rails.
    - SECURITY.oot: Capability tokens, sandbox boundaries, and op matrices.
    - WORKFLOW.oot: Where to write, OODA loop rhythm, Multi-Agent Swarm Protocol, and bug lore.
    - QUICKREF.oot: Universal AI quick reference for syntax, types, stdlib, and verification laws.

### 1.17 Power Law 80/20 Leverage (Pillar 4)
  Focus compiler and runtime architecture on the 20% high-leverage primitives providing 80% of systemic capability:
    - SSA mem2reg variable promotion.
    - Flat closures with explicit capture environments.
    - Direct in-process ELF64 and WASM machine code emission.
    - Linear arena allocators with bulk resets for zero-heap fast paths.

### 1.18 Blue Ocean Strategy & Sovereign Moat (Pillar 5)
  Defend openOODA's uncontested sovereign moat across three foundational vectors:
    - Compile-time SMT invariant check elision: prove contract postconditions statically to elide runtime checks.
    - Zero ambient authority capability security model: 14 explicit unforgeable capability tokens.
    - Zero-toolchain self-hosted bootstrapping: pure .oo directly emitting ELF64 and WASM binaries without external C compilers.

### 1.19 .oot File Naming Convention (Provenance by Case)
  Two naming tiers exist for `.oot` files. The tier is visible at a glance.

  ALLCAPS.oot — Owner-authored canonical files only.
    - The 10 canonical process boards (START, RULES, NORTHSTAR, etc.).
    - RFC documents in `rfcs/` (e.g. `0001-name.oot` is lowercase — that is correct; RFCs are agent-co-authored).
    - Any file a human author writes and is responsible for.

  lowercase.oot — LLM-generated files only.
    - All files in `audit/` and `.agents/` directories.
    - Wave logs, session rollups, audit reports, swarm state files.
    - Any file an agent creates autonomously without explicit owner authorship.

  The rule is simple: if a human did not write it and own it, it is lowercase.
  This makes provenance permanently readable from the filename alone.
  `check_board.oo` only whitelists the 10 ALLCAPS canonical boards.
  LLM-generated lowercase files must stay inside `audit/` or `.agents/`.

## 2. Planning rules

  Focus: choose at most 5 tasks at one time.
  File size: keep files strictly <= 256 lines.
  Sprint alignment: pick active tasks directly from ROADMAP.oot.
  Execution rhythm: follow the Observe-Orient-Decide-Act tempo in WORKFLOW.oot.
  Task order:
    1. Fix broken code first.
    2. Fix security holes next (see SECURITY.oot).
    3. Build the next planned feature (from ROADMAP.oot).
    4. Clean up messy code last.
